To place an icon inside an input and make it clickable, the cleanest approach is to wrap the `<input>` and the `<button>` containing the icon inside a relative `div`.

Here is a common, modern solution using HTML and CSS.

### 1. The Structure (HTML)

We wrap the input and button in a container with `position: relative`.

```html
<div class="search-container">
  <input type="text" id="search-input" placeholder="Search...">
  <button id="search-button" onclick="handleSearch()">
    <!-- Your SVG Icon -->
    <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2">
      <circle cx="11" cy="11" r="8"></circle>
      <line x1="21" y1="21" x2="16.65" y2="16.65"></line>
    </svg>
  </button>
</div>
```

### 2. The Styling (CSS)

The trick here is to position the button absolutely to the right side of the container, while adding padding to the input so the text doesn't hide behind the button.

```css
.search-container {
  position: relative;
  display: inline-block;
  width: 300px; /* Adjust as needed */
}

#search-input {
  width: 100%;
  padding: 10px 40px 10px 10px; /* Note: The 40px right padding keeps text from hitting the icon */
  box-sizing: border-box;
}

#search-button {
  position: absolute;
  right: 5px;
  top: 50%;
  transform: translateY(-50%);
  border: none;
  background: none;
  cursor: pointer;
  padding: 5px;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
}
```

### 3. The Functionality (JavaScript)

Finally, create the function to grab the value from the input.

```javascript
function handleSearch() {
  const input = document.getElementById('search-input');
  const value = input.value;

  if (value.trim() !== "") {
    console.log("Searching for:", value);
    // Add your search logic here
  } else {
    alert("Please enter something first!");
  }
}
```

---

### If you are using React:

If you are using React, the logic is slightly cleaner because you use `state`.

```jsx
import React, { useState } from 'react';

const SearchBar = () => {
  const [query, setQuery] = useState("");

  const handleSearch = () => {
    console.log("Searching for:", query);
  };

  return (
    <div style={{ position: 'relative', display: 'inline-block' }}>
      <input 
        value={query}
        onChange={(e) => setQuery(e.target.value)}
        style={{ paddingRight: '40px' }} 
      />
      <button 
        onClick={handleSearch}
        style={{ position: 'absolute', right: '5px', top: '5px', border: 'none', background: 'none' }}
      >
        <svg /* ... your svg code ... */>...</svg>
      </button>
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Why this is the best approach:
1.  **Accessibility:** Using a `<button>` specifically for the icon ensures that screen readers and keyboard users can interact with it (it remains focusable).
2.  **Padding:** The `padding-right` on the input ensures the user doesn't type *under* the icon.
3.  **Positioning:** `position: absolute` on the button allows it to "float" inside the input box regardless of how wide the main input becomes.

